codex
Both passing variants are excellent on the bench. The sorted-list variant is the most faithful — it maintains the exact same stable sorted order as `sorted()`, works with any orderable (even unhashable) values, and even preserves tie object-identity semantics. The Fenwick variant is O(n log n) for pathological giant windows, but at the bench sizes the sorted-list is 2× faster and the semantics are literally the same computation, just incremental. I'll go with:

- `rolling_median`: maintain a sorted window list with C-backed `bisect` insert/remove instead of re-sorting each window.
- `window_distinct_counts`: slide a running frequency dict instead of rebuilding a `set` per window.
apply patch
